[Unit]
Description=Refresh ephor feeds, reopen work whose item has moved, and start what is due

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# Refresh first: sync compares the item against the snapshot taken when its
# work was dispatched, so it needs the item as it is now (§FS-005-dispatch.5).
# Then the sweep, which starts a run only where a recipe asked to run itself
# and no run is live on the root (§FS-005-dispatch.24). Work that said nothing
# about this is still nobody's to start but the reader's: `ephor work run`
# without `--due` remains deliberate, and this line never reaches it.
#
# `work sync` runs the same sweep itself for what it just reopened, so this is
# the backstop rather than the trigger: it catches work born anywhere else — a
# ticket a hand appended, another ephor, a root whose run died. The sweep reads
# the world and starts nothing on a root that already has a run, so running it
# here and there both costs one lock probe per root.
#
# `--act` on both sweeps: a mutating verb whose resolved scope names more than
# one project reports what it would do and writes nothing unless it is told to
# act (§FS-011-command-line.10). This unit is exactly the case the flag is for
# — a sweep with nobody watching, over every project the site watches — so it
# says so once here rather than being silently exempt. `refresh` reads and
# needs no such word.
ExecStart=%h/.cargo/bin/ephor refresh --quiet
ExecStart=%h/.cargo/bin/ephor work sync --act
ExecStart=%h/.cargo/bin/ephor work run --due --act
